// LIMITATION P3: f64 array-element updates inside a non-main function are
// silently dropped past a hard cap of 256. `bump` performs 257 read-modify-
// write updates `a[0] = a[0] + 1.0`; expected output 257.0.
// Observed (2026-08-02, Madaros v0.80.0): 256.0 -- update #257 never takes
// effect; no warning from `souc check` or `souc build`.
// Control: p3_f64_store_control.sio (255 updates) prints 255.0.
// Notes: this is the statement-kind-dependent face of the silent per-function
// truncation (LIMITATION P1). Measured thresholds in this build:
//   x = 7              (scalar store)     >702 statements survive
//   x = x + 1          (scalar RMW)       cap 316 (317th dropped)
//   a[i] = 1.0         (f64 elem store)   >259 survive
//   a[0] = a[0] + 1    (i64 elem RMW)     >302 survive
//   a[0] = a[0] + 1.0  (f64 elem RMW)     cap 256 (257th dropped) <- lowest
// Round-6 observed this as "f64 array-element assignment outside main is a
// silent no-op" because f64 element RMWs hit the truncation first.
fn bump(a: &![f64; 1]) with Mut {
    a[0] = a[0] + 1.0
    a[0] = a[0] + 1.0
